MASTER PROJECT PROMPT — AGRISENSE AI

I want to design and develop a complete hardware-centered AI-powered agricultural technology platform called “AgriSense AI” for Smart India Hackathon (SIH) 2026. The project is developed by Team HardHackers from RMK College of Engineering and Technology, Department of EE–VLSI. Our SIH 2026 Problem Statement ID is PSH4 and the official problem statement is “Smart Agricultural Irrigation and Crop Monitoring System”. The given problem statement asks us to develop an IoT-based agricultural system that monitors soil moisture, temperature, humidity and other environmental parameters and automatically controls irrigation according to crop requirements. However, AgriSense AI should not be designed as a basic automatic irrigation project. The solution must understand the larger real-world agricultural problems faced by farmers and build an integrated platform that helps farmers monitor farms, manage irrigation, understand crop conditions, identify possible crop problems, request expert inspection, use drone-based visual inspection, receive AI-assisted agricultural intelligence, and manage the complete problem-resolution process from one platform. The core philosophy of AgriSense AI is: “The farmer should not have to learn the technology; the technology should understand the farmer.”

The system should be designed as an AI-powered multi-farm, multi-field, multi-crop agricultural management platform. The platform should support an Admin, multiple Farmers and multiple Technicians. A farmer can have multiple farms, each farm can contain multiple fields, each field can contain different crops, irrigation zones, soil sensors, environmental sensors, water-level monitoring and motors. The system must be scalable so that it can start with a small demonstration setup but later support many farmers, farms, fields, crops, sensors, motors, technicians and inspection cases. The complete architecture should connect physical hardware, ESP32, sensors, irrigation motors, real-time database, web dashboard, AI services, voice interaction, drone imagery, technician inspection, notifications and agricultural product management.

The primary hardware layer should use ESP32-based IoT devices connected to agricultural sensors. The system should monitor parameters such as soil moisture, temperature, humidity, water level and other relevant environmental parameters. Sensor data should be continuously sent to the real-time database through the ESP32 and should be displayed on the AgriSense AI dashboard. The farmer and Admin should be able to see live values, sensor health, last update time, field condition, motor status and irrigation status. Soil moisture should be represented clearly as a percentage and the system should be able to determine whether the soil is sufficiently wet, requires irrigation or is approaching a critical dry condition. The irrigation system should contain multiple irrigation zones and multiple motors or pumps. Each motor should be associated with a particular field or irrigation zone. The system should automatically control irrigation according to configured crop requirements, soil moisture thresholds and irrigation rules. At the same time, authorized users should be able to manually control motors. The system must keep safety and deterministic hardware control separate from AI reasoning. AI can recommend irrigation or interpret the farmer’s request, but actual motor control must pass through authorization, hardware-status checks, water-availability checks, sensor-health checks, fault checks and suitable confirmation or predefined safe rules before the motor is activated.

A major feature of AgriSense AI should be an AI Voice Assistant designed especially for farmers. Many farmers may not know which menu to open, which sensor to check or which button to press. Therefore, instead of forcing the farmer to understand the technical interface, the farmer should be able to communicate naturally with the platform using voice. The farmer should be able to ask questions such as “What is the temperature today?”, “What is the humidity?”, “What is the weather for the next five days?”, “What is the soil moisture in Field 1?”, “Which field needs water?”, “How is my tomato crop?”, “How much water did I use today?”, “Is Motor 2 running?”, “What are today’s alerts?”, “What happened in my last inspection?” and “What is the status of my order?”. The AI Voice Assistant should convert speech to text, understand the farmer’s intent, identify the required farm, field, crop or motor, retrieve information from the appropriate system and respond in simple farmer-friendly language. It should also be capable of triggering authorized platform actions. For example, if the farmer says “Turn on Motor 2 for 20 minutes”, the AI should understand the command, identify Motor 2, check whether the user has permission, verify that the motor and sensor system are functioning, verify water availability and other safety conditions, ask for confirmation when required, and then send the command to the database and ESP32 control layer. The flow should be Voice → Speech-to-Text → AI Intent Understanding → Authorization/Safety Validation → Database/Control Command → ESP32 → Relay/Driver → Motor → Real-Time Status Update. The voice assistant should not blindly execute dangerous physical actions.

AgriSense AI should include weather intelligence because weather directly affects irrigation and crop health. The platform should integrate a suitable weather service and provide current temperature, humidity, rainfall probability, weather condition, wind information and multi-day forecasts. The farmer should be able to ask weather-related questions using voice. The AI should also combine weather information with IoT sensor data when generating agricultural recommendations. For example, if rain is expected and the soil moisture is already sufficient, the system can warn that irrigation may not be required. If the temperature is high, humidity is low and soil moisture is falling, the system can identify possible water stress and recommend checking irrigation. Weather should therefore not be shown only as a separate weather page; it should become part of the overall farm intelligence system.

The platform should provide continuous crop monitoring. Each crop should be associated with its field, crop type, planting date, growth stage and crop history. AgriSense AI should combine soil moisture, temperature, humidity, weather, irrigation history and crop information to provide a farm and crop health overview. The system should display crop health status and provide an understandable Farm Health Score based on available data such as soil condition, environmental conditions, irrigation status, water availability, sensor health, crop condition and inspection results. The AI should explain the reasons behind the health score instead of showing only a number. The platform should also include an AI Crop Advisor that can provide crop suggestions based on available information such as soil type, season, climate, water availability, previous crop, crop history and environmental conditions. It should also support crop rotation and next-crop recommendations. These recommendations must be presented as decision support and should not be represented as guaranteed agricultural outcomes.

One of the major innovations of AgriSense AI should be the integration of drone-based crop inspection with AI Vision. This feature should specifically address the problem of visible crop pests, worms, caterpillars, insects, leaf damage and other visible crop abnormalities. The drone should not be automatically dispatched by AI. The workflow must intentionally include a human technician. If a farmer notices a problem, the farmer can report it through the website or simply speak to the AI Voice Assistant. For example, the farmer can say: “There is a problem in my tomato field, please check it.” The AI should understand the request and create an inspection request containing the farmer, farm, field, crop and problem description. This request should be sent to the Admin. The Admin should review the request and assign a technician BEFORE any drone inspection takes place. This order is an important part of the system architecture: Farmer Problem Report → Admin → Technician Assignment → Technician Farm Visit → Drone Inspection.

The Admin dashboard should contain an Inspection Request Management section. Whenever a farmer reports a problem, the Admin should receive a notification and be able to view the farmer name, farm, field, crop, problem description, request date and priority. The Admin should then select an appropriate technician and assign the inspection. The technician should receive the assignment through the Technician Dashboard. The assignment should contain the farm, field, crop, reported problem, farmer information and inspection type. The technician then physically visits the farm and performs the inspection. The technician uses a camera-equipped drone to fly over the relevant field and capture images of the crop, leaves and affected areas. Multiple images can be captured from different zones of the field. The images should be uploaded to AgriSense AI and stored securely for inspection history.

After the technician uploads the drone images, the AI Vision module should analyze the images. The AI should attempt to identify visible agricultural problems such as worms, caterpillars, visible insects, pest damage, chewed leaves, holes in leaves, damaged leaves, discoloration, visible crop stress, unhealthy crop areas, weeds, dry-looking areas, water-stressed areas and other visible abnormalities. The AI should identify the crop if possible, identify the affected field and zone where possible, estimate the severity and provide a confidence score. The result should be structured and easy for the technician to understand. For example: Crop: Tomato; Field: Field 2; Zone: B3; Possible Problem: Caterpillar/Worm Damage; AI Confidence: 87%; Severity: Moderate; Status: Technician Verification Required. The AI must not claim that the image analysis is a guaranteed diagnosis. It should use responsible language such as “possible pest detected”, “suspected worm damage”, “possible caterpillar infestation” or “visible pest damage”. The AI is an assistant for the technician, not a replacement for physical agricultural inspection.

After receiving the AI result, the assigned technician must physically inspect the affected area. The technician should check the actual plants, leaves, insects, worms or damage and compare the physical condition with the AI result. The technician should be able to upload additional photographs, add observations, confirm the AI result, reject the AI result, identify the actual problem and specify severity and affected area. For example, the AI may detect “Possible Caterpillar Damage – 87% confidence”, and the technician may verify “Confirmed – Moderate Severity – Field 2 Zone B3”. The technician should then submit a detailed inspection report. The report should contain the farmer, farm, field, crop, AI result, AI confidence, technician verification, severity, affected area, images, technician observations and recommended next action. The report should then be sent to the Admin for review.

The Admin should have a complete view of the inspection case, including the original farmer request, technician assignment, drone images, AI analysis, AI confidence, technician images, physical verification and final inspection report. If the problem is confirmed and a treatment or agricultural product is required, the Admin should be able to manage the next stage through AgriSense AI. The platform should include an agricultural product management and ordering module. This can include appropriate agricultural supplies such as fertilizers, soil amendments, seeds, approved pest-management products and other agricultural supplies. However, AI should not independently prescribe or purchase chemical products. The safe workflow should be AI Detection → Technician Verification → Admin Review → Approved Action/Product → Product Selection → Order. The Admin can select the required product, quantity, delivery details and place the order through the same web platform. The farmer should be able to see the order status and receive notifications. The order lifecycle can include Order Placed, Processing, Shipped, Delivered and Completed/Resolved.

The platform should create a complete Case Management system. Every reported agricultural problem should receive a unique case ID. The case should contain the farmer, farm, field, crop, reported problem, request date, assigned technician, drone inspection status, AI analysis, AI confidence, technician verification, severity, affected zone, inspection report, approved action, product order and final resolution. Once the issue is handled, the case should be marked Resolved. This entire case should remain in the farm history so that future decisions can use historical information. The system should maintain long-term farm history including crop cycles, previous crops, irrigation events, soil moisture trends, temperature, humidity, weather conditions, motor usage, water usage, pest incidents, drone images, AI analysis, technician reports, treatments, product orders and resolved cases.

AgriSense AI should combine data from multiple sources instead of relying on one sensor or one AI image. For example, if the soil moisture sensor shows low moisture, temperature is high, rainfall probability is low and the drone image shows visible crop stress in the same field zone, the AI can identify a possible water-stress condition and recommend physical verification or irrigation according to configured rules. If the drone detects possible pest damage and the technician confirms it, that event should become part of the crop history. This combination of IoT data, weather information, drone vision, AI analysis and human verification should be one of the main strengths of AgriSense AI.

The platform should also include a powerful notification system. Farmers should receive alerts for low soil moisture, irrigation events, sensor failures, motor faults, technician assignment, inspection progress, inspection completion, confirmed crop problems, order updates and important weather conditions. Admins should receive alerts for new farmer requests, sensor failures, motor faults, new inspection requests, technician assignments, AI inspection results, technician reports, confirmed pest cases and order events. Technicians should receive notifications for new assignments, inspection reminders, farm visits and relevant Admin instructions. Notifications should be available inside the web platform and should be designed so that important alerts are clearly visible.

The Farmer Dashboard should be simple, visual and farmer-friendly. It should include Dashboard, My Farms, Fields, Live Monitoring, Irrigation Control, Crops, AI Crop Advisor, AI Voice Assistant, Weather, Crop Inspection, Technician Requests, Orders, Notifications, Farm History, Reports and Settings. The dashboard should show total farms, total fields, active motors, average soil moisture, temperature, humidity, water used today, water saved and Farm Health Score. The Admin Dashboard should include Dashboard, Farmers, Farms, Fields, Crops, Technicians, Inspection Requests, Technician Assignments, AI Inspection, Products, Orders, Notifications, Reports, System Monitoring and Settings. It should show total farmers, total farms, active fields, active motors, pending inspections, active technicians, confirmed pest cases and pending orders. The Technician Dashboard should include Dashboard, My Assignments, Farm Visits, Drone Inspection, AI Image Analysis, Inspection Reports, History, Notifications and Profile, with cards for assigned inspections, pending visits, completed inspections and confirmed cases.

The platform should contain an interactive farm map that displays farm locations, field boundaries, irrigation zones, sensor locations, motor locations and inspection zones. A field can be divided into zones so that AI and technicians can identify affected areas more precisely. For example, Field 2 can be represented as a grid of zones such as A1, A2, A3, B1, B2, B3, C1, C2 and C3, with green representing healthy, yellow representing attention required and red representing a detected or confirmed problem. This will make drone inspection results much more understandable to the farmer and Admin.

The complete system should use a scalable database structure with entities such as Users, Farmers, Admins, Technicians, Farms, Fields, Crops, Sensors, Motors, Irrigation Zones, Sensor Readings, Weather Data, Inspections, Drone Images, AI Analysis Results, Technician Reports, Products, Orders, Notifications, Crop History and Cases. The system should use secure authentication and role-based access control. Farmers should only access their own farms and data, technicians should access their assigned inspection cases and authorized information, and Admins should have management-level access. Credentials and sensitive configuration values must never be exposed in frontend code.

The technology architecture should be hardware-focused because this project is being developed by the EE–VLSI department. The physical system should use ESP32, soil-moisture sensors, temperature/humidity sensors, water-level sensing, relay or motor control and irrigation motors/pumps. The web platform can use a modern frontend architecture such as HTML/CSS/JavaScript or React, with Firebase Authentication, Firebase Realtime Database or Firestore and Firebase Storage as appropriate. ESP32 should communicate sensor values and receive authorized irrigation commands. Drone images should be uploaded to secure storage and passed to an AI Vision service or model for analysis. A speech-to-text service and AI language model can power the Voice Assistant. A suitable weather API should provide live weather and forecast information. A map library such as Leaflet can be used for farm and field visualization. The architecture should be modular so that the AI model, drone system, sensors, database or hardware can be upgraded later without rebuilding the entire platform.

The system should be designed as a real-world prototype suitable for SIH 2026. Where actual drone hardware, AI models, weather APIs or ESP32 hardware are not available during demonstration, the platform should support a clearly marked simulation/demo mode using simulated sensor values, sample drone images, simulated AI results, simulated technician assignments and sample product orders. The final demonstration should show an end-to-end scenario rather than disconnected features. A strong demo scenario should begin with a farmer using voice to report a problem in a tomato field. AgriSense AI should create the inspection request, notify the Admin, allow the Admin to assign a technician, show the technician receiving the assignment, allow the technician to upload drone images, show AI analysis identifying possible worm/caterpillar damage, show the technician physically verifying the result, generate the inspection report, allow the Admin to review it, select an appropriate approved agricultural product and place an order, notify the farmer and finally mark the case as resolved and store it in farm history. Another demo scenario should show the farmer asking about weather and soil moisture and then using voice to request irrigation, followed by the ESP32 controlling the motor and updating the dashboard in real time.

The most important innovation of AgriSense AI is not any single feature but the integration of all these technologies into one continuous agricultural workflow. The system should connect Farmer → Voice AI → IoT → Smart Irrigation → Crop Monitoring → Problem Reporting → Admin → Technician Assignment → Drone Inspection → AI Vision → Pest/Worm Detection → Human Verification → Admin Review → Agricultural Product Ordering → Resolution → Farm History → Future AI Intelligence. The farmer should be able to communicate naturally, the IoT system should continuously observe the physical farm, the AI should interpret data and images, the Admin should coordinate operations, the technician should provide human verification, the drone should provide aerial visual information, and the platform should manage the resolution process. The goal is not simply to automate one agricultural task but to create a connected intelligent agricultural ecosystem.

The final project should therefore be positioned as: “AgriSense AI – An AI-powered IoT agricultural management platform that enables smart irrigation, real-time crop monitoring, farmer-friendly voice interaction, technician-assisted drone inspection, AI-based pest and worm detection, human verification, weather intelligence, agricultural product management and complete farm history.” The project should remain strongly aligned with SIH 2026 Problem Statement PSH4 by keeping smart irrigation and crop monitoring as the core foundation, while extending the solution with AI, voice interaction, drone inspection, technician management and agricultural problem resolution. The final product should look like a professional AgriTech platform rather than a basic college website, and every feature must be logically connected to the central goal of helping farmers reduce water wastage, monitor crop conditions, identify agricultural problems earlier, access expert support and make better farm-management decisions.

